 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public authorities law, in relation to webcasting of public meetings",
    "basePrintNo": "S1854",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S1854-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§1002 & 1020-c, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S1854\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the public authorities law, in\nrelation to webcasting of public meetings\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThis bill requires the New York Power Authority and Long Island Power\nAuthority to webcast all public meetings and to make a recording of\nthe meeting available on their website for a period of at least one\nyear.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection one and two require the New York Power Authority and the Long\nIsland Power Authority to webcast all public meetings, to make a\nrecording of the meeting available via the authority's website for a\nperiod of at least one year, and to archive the recording so that it\ncan be available upon request after the one-year retention period.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThis bill will improve accountability and transparency within the New\nYork Power Authority and the Long Island Power Authority. Currently,\nboth power authorities provide a live webcast of their meetings. They\nare required to do so pursuant to an Executive Order, not statute.\nFurther, they are not required to make a copy of the recording\navailable to the public after the meeting occurs. This causes a\nhardship for our residents and advocates who are not able to watch the\nproceedings live, and instead prefer to watch the proceeding at a time\nthat is convenient to them.\n\nCurrently, NYPA has a policy of providing a link to the recording on\ntheir webpage for only thirty days. The meeting is then archived and\navailable, upon request, for a period of three months. This policy\nmakes it difficult for the public to hold the members of the Board\naccountable, since there is no record of proceedings available after\nthe three-month period.\n\nLIPA provides the public with access to recordings of Board Meetings,\nfor a period of one year. However, the website they maintain does not\nindicate what happens to the recording after the one-year period.\n\nBy making these recordings more accessible to the public, and\nrequiring that the authorities archive and retain copies of their\nmeetings, we can ensure that complete records of these proceedings are\nmaintained.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew Bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone to the state\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect 30 days after becoming law.\n",
